    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d307">

                <pb facs="0504" n="488" xml:id="pg488"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.5200/3–1649: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Ray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">niact</hi>
                        
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-03-16T13:00:00-03:00">March 16, 1949—1
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>259. Referring reported meeting between President Truman, General Marshall<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d307-f1"/> and Ambassador Pawley<anchor n="2" xml:id="d307-f2"
                    /> to discuss ECA purchases and other possible
                    means of helping Argentina straighten out dollar shortage and financial
                        difficulties.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d307-f3"/> Greenup<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d307-f4"/> and I feel Embtels
                    230, March 9 and 248 of March 12 as well as Embassy airgrams 113<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d307-f5"/> and 134 should be considered before announcing any plans
                        ECA purchases or other assistance. Also
                    please see other communications on general subject including letters to
                    Tewksbury and Bruce.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d307-f6"/> We hear persistent reports
                    Argentine Government feels, threat of expropriation may spur us into taking
                    action. Argentines show no disposition make efforts settle at reduced prices.
                    They are insisting on pound sterling convertibility.</p>
                <p>We firmly believe it is to our interest help Argentina but before actually doing
                    so we should reach an understanding which will afford adequate protection to
                    American properties and business interests. We have discussed overall situation
                    with Arnold, Whitney and other Americans as well as diplomats and prominent
                    Argentines all of whom are extremely pessimistic. Government has gone back on
                    promise remove 20 percent tax on meat exports and is showing generally
                    unreasonable attitude.</p>
                <p>I repeat we believe necessary clear up situation and help Argentina but think any
                    precipitate action or announcement would be disadvantageous since we can gain
                    more by negotiating before extending help.</p>
                <p>Please inform James Bruce urgently.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d307-f1" n="1" xml:id="d307-f1-content">
                <p>Former Secretary of State George C. Marshall.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d307-f2" n="2" xml:id="d307-f2-content">
                <p>William D. Pawley had been U.S. Ambassador in Brazil, 1946–1948.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d307-f3" n="3" xml:id="d307-f3-content">
                <p>A brief summary of Mr. Pawley’s meeting with President Truman is contained in
                    Department telegram 205, March 23, to Buenos Aires, <ref target="#pg490">p.
                        490</ref>. According to a memorandum of conversation, March 11, by the Chief
                    of the Division of River Plate Affairs (Tewksbury), Foreign Minister Bramuglia
                    had sent his nephew, Sohar R. del Campo, to the United States with personal
                    letters (not printed) for General Marshall and Mr. Pawley, apparently in an
                    effort to obtain help from the United States in solving Argentina’s present
                    economic problems. (835.50/3–1149).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d307-f4" n="4" xml:id="d307-f4-content">
                <p>Julian C. Greenup, Counselor of Embassy for Economic Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d307-f5" n="5" xml:id="d307-f5-content">
                <p>Telegram 248 and airgram 113 are not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d307-f6" n="6" xml:id="d307-f6-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
